//! Theme-level port of the PR #26 reference `tests/focus_gutter.rs`
//! (f4867e9b5f8a08a3bb61ce80d644198a29f51984).
//!
//! The reference drives whole widgets (Button, ListBox, TextInput); the parity
//! port pins the shared theme API those widgets must adopt
//! (`gutter_symbol`, `gutter`, `chosen_in`, `inactive_look`, `legible_mark`).
//! Widget-level adoption is tracked as a handoff: controls currently paint
//! gutters through the recipe system (`Part::GUTTER`) and apps hand-paint
//! `▎`.

//! The focus-bar policy table, measured against the frozen widget output.
//!
//! Truth table: `crates/termrock-controls/tests/frozen_ink_pins.rs` and
//! `crates/termrock-navigation/tests/frozen_ink_pins.rs` (commit-scoped
//! audit, 2026-10-21). The audit found no single baked-in rule reproducing
//! every family, so the rule is a [`FocusBarPolicy`] parameter:
//!
//! - `BUTTON` draws the bar only while focused AND focus-visible, and a
//!   focus-visible bar survives the disabled state (truecolor/256/16/mono).
//! - `CHOICE`/`TREE`/`STEPS` rows draw the bar while focused even when
//!   disabled at the truecolor, 256 and 16 tiers; the mono tier blanks it.
//! - `LIST` rows blank the bar while disabled at every tier.
//! - No family needs focus visibility for a focused row, and no family draws
//!   a bar without focus.

//! Adoption example: the upgraded helpers reproduce the frozen widget ink.
//!
//! For every frozen paint site the audit measured (migration matrix,
//! 2026-10-21; truth tables in `crates/termrock-controls/tests/
//! frozen_ink_pins.rs` and `crates/termrock-navigation/tests/
//! frozen_ink_pins.rs`), this renders the same gutter cell twice — once
//! through the frozen recipe path (`Theme::resolve`) and once through the
//! upgraded helpers (`Theme::focus_bar_symbol` + `Theme::gutter_in` with
//! `GutterCell::Inherit`) — into a one-cell buffer and asserts the cells
//! equal. Marker ink goes through `Theme::chosen_in_with` with
//! `BackdropSelection::Accent`. A controls/navigation paint site can now
//! adopt the helpers byte-identically at these states.

//! Gutter-cell fill policy: inherit the container or stamp an explicit blank.
//!
//! Frozen evidence: `crates/termrock-controls/tests/frozen_ink_pins.rs` and
//! `crates/termrock-navigation/tests/frozen_ink_pins.rs` pin the bar cell's
//! `bg == None` (the container background shows through) and the audit notes
//! the helpers' explicit `fg=bg` stamp as the byte-difference that blocked
//! adoption (matrix, 2026-10-21). [`GutterCell::Inherit`] is the frozen path;
//! [`GutterCell::Fill`] reproduces the reference behaviour.

/// Reveal a span of `len` rows starting at `start`, keeping `context`
/// rows of context visible when the viewport allows it. A span at least
/// as long as the viewport aligns its start with the top (ported from
/// PR #26 `src/core/scroll.rs::reveal_span`).

/// Reveal the cursor according to the follow policy. With
/// [`FollowCursor::OnMove`], repeating the same cursor in the same
/// viewport is a no-op, so a repaint never snaps a wheeled view back
/// (ported from PR #26 `src/core/scroll.rs::follow`).

/// Press the track with an explicit [`TrackClick`] policy: `Jump` puts
/// the thumb under the pointer, `Page` moves one viewport toward the
/// pointer and starts a paging drag (ported from PR #26
/// `src/core/scroll.rs::press_track_with`).

//! Executable consumer exercising the shell crate through the public
//! Termrock APIs only: the [`termrock_shell::AppShell`] frame, a
//! [`termrock_shell::Router`] of pages, and the [`termrock_shell::KeyRouter`]
//! key tiers, with unrelated domain data (a file-browser trail).

//! Chrome geometry: how the shell splits its frame into header, body and
//! footer regions.
//!
//! Ported from PR #26 `src/shell/chrome.rs` (commit `f4867e9b5`). The source's
//! `Menus<C>` command map and `Chrome::fit` dialog inset are not ported: the
//! parity menu bar resolves choices through [`termrock_core::action::ActionKey`]
//! itself, and the parity dialog owns its chrome through its own layout, so
//! both belong to their component crates rather than to the shell.

/// Split `area` once into the three regions. The strips keep their rows
/// while the area is tall enough; the body takes what is left and is
/// empty when nothing is.

//! The footer's ownership rules: which hints it names, in what order, and
//! what the status line does to them.
//!
//! Ported from PR #26 `src/shell/footer.rs` (commit `f4867e9b5`). The parity
//! adaptation keeps the compose rules and drops the rendering: the parity
//! hint bar is `termrock_feedback::HintBar`, which renders a runtime
//! [`termrock_runtime::keymap::HintLayer`]; an app maps the composed
//! [`Hint`]s onto that layer at adoption time. The source's `Kind` status
//! variant and the peek (`ui::reveal`) integration are not ported: parity
//! status tone is a theme [`Role`] and the reveal machinery belongs to the
//! runtime's feedback wave.

/// The footer: the hints it names and the status message it carries.
///
/// Compose rules, ported exactly: the topmost layer that offers hints wins
/// over the fallback set; Tab-next is added once and never while a text
/// field is editing; hints never name a key the terminal cannot deliver;
/// a dirty pin holds the commit key's place; an error demotes every hint
/// and takes the status line.

/// Fold one more layer in underneath what is already set: the first
/// layer that offers hints wins, and the fallback shows only when no
/// layer did.

//! Keyboard deliverability: which keys a terminal can actually deliver, so
//! hints never advertise a chord the app will never see.
//!
//! Ported from PR #26 `src/core/keyboard.rs` (commit `f4867e9b5`), delivery
//! observation and the label filter only. The Kitty-protocol push/pop session
//! sequences stay with the session crate on parity, which owns terminal modes.

/// May a hint with this label be advertised? A Shift+click or Shift+drag is
/// never keyboard-deliverable; a Cmd or Alt chord only when the terminal
/// delivers that modifier.

//! The application shell: routing, navigation, chrome, quit guard, status and
//! footer ownership, ported from the PR #26 shell layer onto the parity
//! runtime contracts (`App`/`Cx`/`Ui`/`Response`).
//!
//! The parity runtime already owns focus, hit testing, pointer routing, layers
//! and the update loop, so this crate owns only what apps still carry per app:
//! the page router, the navigation stack, chrome geometry, the quit guard, the
//! status line, the footer hint vocabulary and keyboard-deliverability, and the
//! reachability harness used to prove every word stays reachable.
//!
//! Nothing here draws its own pixels: the shell composes through the shared
//! composition slots ([`shell::AppShell`]) and returns vocabulary the app
//! renders with existing components. The one exception is
//! [`report::TextReport`], a content block that paints through the runtime
//! [`termrock_runtime::Ui`] like the other Termrock blocks do.
//!
//! The `reach` module is a test tool compiled only with the crate's
//! default-off `testing` feature, so production builds never carry it.

//! Page routing: the navigation stack, tabs and page identity.
//!
//! Ported from PR #26 `src/shell/nav.rs` (commit `f4867e9b5`). The parity
//! adaptation returns [`Option`] where the source asserted internal
//! invariants, and the breadcrumb is the shell-owned [`Crumb`] model because
//! the parity breadcrumb widget is owned by another crate wave.

/// Opaque identity of the page currently shown. Focus upkeep compares keys to
/// detect a page change; two visits to the same route get different keys so a
/// re-entered page re-focuses its primary control.

/// Per-tab navigation stacks with one active tab.
///
/// Each entry keeps a monotonically increasing key so focus upkeep can tell a
/// new page from a revisit.

/// A router with one tab per root. At least one root is required.
///
/// # Panics
/// Panics when `roots` is empty.

/// The active stack, mutably. `None` when the active tab is gone.
///
/// A router always keeps its active tab, so the live case is the only
/// one the app sees; the accessor stays total so a broken invariant
/// degrades to "no active stack" instead of panicking.

/// A click on breadcrumb segment `hit` maps to the pop that shows it.
/// `levels` extra segments the shell renders above this router's stack
/// shift the mapping the same way they do for [`Crumb::on_click`].

//! The page contract: what one routed screen owes the shell.
//!
//! Ported from PR #26 `src/shell/page.rs` (commit `f4867e9b5`). The parity
//! adaptation collapses the source's `PageTypes` associated-type machinery:
//! a page updates against the runtime's [`termrock_runtime::cx::Cx`] (it
//! drains its own intents) and draws as a composition leaf. The source's
//! `row_anchor`, `open_context` and `row_choice` helpers are not ported:
//! they stage context menus through the source's `LayerTag`/`LayerResult`
//! machinery, which the parity overlay layer (`RenderCtx::overlay`,
//! `LayerSpec`) replaces; that port belongs to the overlay extraction wave.
//! Focus blur, ticks and messages arrive as intents here rather than as
//! separate trait methods.

/// Drain the page's intents and update. Keys, pastes, pointer events,
/// focus changes and layer events arrive here as intents for the page's
/// owner ids.

/// Draw into the shell-resolved body bounds.
///
/// Existing pages keep their `draw` implementation. Pages that need
/// layout-relative cursor or control coordinates can override this hook
/// and use `body` for registration and drawing.

/// Replay a key as if typed: a menu choice or hint click names a character
/// and the shell turns it into the key event the page would have seen.
///
/// Ported from PR #26 `page.rs::replay_key`.

/// Rebuild a page on tick only when its view actually changed, so idle
/// animation frames stay idle.
///
/// Ported from PR #26 `page.rs::rebuild_on_tick`.

//! The quit guard: what the app stands to lose when it leaves, and the
//! confirmation that protects it.
//!
//! Ported from PR #26 `src/ui/leave.rs` (commit `f4867e9b5`). The parity
//! adaptation describes the confirmation as a [`LeaveDialog`] (kind, title,
//! body and the resolved meaning of each action) instead of building the
//! source's `Dialog` widget directly, because the parity dialog is owned by
//! the overlays crate and resolves through `ActionKey`s; the app maps the
//! guard's [`LeaveDialogKind`] onto whatever dialog component it renders.

/// Which confirmation the guard wants shown, and what each action means.
///
/// The app renders this with its own dialog component; the meanings of the
/// actions follow [`LeaveDialogKind`] exactly.

//! The reachability harness: does every word a screen shows at a generous
//! size stay reachable at every shipping size?
//!
//! Ported from PR #26 `src/shell/reach.rs` (commit `f4867e9b5`). The parity
//! adaptation drives the [`termrock_runtime::Runtime`] directly over a test
//! buffer instead of the source's `Application` trait, and wheels through
//! [`termrock_core::event::MouseKind::Wheel`] (axis plus notch delta) instead
//! of four wheel kinds. The source's `Words::add_overflowed` and the
//! reveal-driven `Reveal` steps are not ported: they read the source's
//! `ui::reveal` overflow log, which the parity feedback wave owns; a case
//! that hides words only behind truncation reveals stays an allow-file row
//! until that wave lands.

/// Check the found reach against the allow file. Every scenario line reads
/// `<app> <scenario> <WxH> <word...> # <reason>`; a fixed row must drop its
/// line and a new row must carry a reason.

//! A plain text report: a heading, an optional subheading, an optional
//! empty-state line and a list of body lines.
//!
//! The block owns the row grid the report paints on and the columns it
//! insets its lines by. The app supplies the words and the policy; nothing
//! here knows about the domain the report describes.
//!
//! It is the multi-line sibling of `termrock_feedback::TextSection`: a
//! section paints a single body line, a report paints a list and an empty
//! state.

/// A plain text report block.
///
/// The default grid puts the heading on the first row, the subheading on the
/// second, the empty state on the third and the body from the fourth, with
/// one column of inset on each side. Every part of that is policy and can be
/// overridden.

/// Paint the report into `area`.
///
/// Every line is clipped to `area`. The subheading and the empty-state
/// line also follow the minimum details height. Body rows stop at the
/// allocated area's bottom edge.

//! Tiered key routing: which keys the shell answers before the page, which
//! the page answers first, and which only fire when nothing else did.
//!
//! Ported from PR #26 `src/shell/router.rs` (commit `f4867e9b5`). The parity
//! adaptation routes on [`termrock_core::event::Key`] chords directly and
//! reports through [`termrock_core::response::Response`], the runtime's
//! update contract, instead of the source's `Outcome`; the focus movement
//! actions use the runtime focus ring's `Cx::focus_next` and `Cx::focus_prev`.
//! Chord matching mirrors the
//! source's `KeyBinding::deliverable` + `lookup` pair: a binding matches an
//! exact key, and a printable key binding stays quiet while a text field
//! owns typing (as do `idle_only` actions). One ordering difference is
//! deliberate: the parity runtime serves Tab and BackTab out of its focus
//! ring before any app code runs, so [`ShellAction::FocusNext`] and
//! [`ShellAction::FocusPrev`] stay in the vocabulary for custom chords an
//! app binds and routes itself, but the default Tab bindings below are
//! documentation of the standard behaviour rather than the mechanism.

/// The shell's key vocabulary, in three tiers.
///
/// `priority` wins over everything the page could do; `keys` are the shell's
/// own bindings, answered when the page did not consume the key; `fallback`
/// fire only when nothing else wanted the key. Printables and `idle_only`
/// actions stay quiet while a text field owns typing.

/// The host a [`KeyRouter`] routes through: the app answers for its layers,
/// menus, pages and commands, and the router supplies the shell's own tiers
/// and quit vocabulary around them.

/// Route a key: a layer above the page answers first, then an open menu
/// bar, then the shell's own tiers around the page. (Hover suppression
/// is the runtime's: on parity the runtime drops hover state on any key
/// press before app code runs, so the router does no hover work.)

//! The app shell block: one header, one body, one footer, laid out through
//! the shared composition machinery or chrome regions.
//!
//! Header and footer strips keep their chrome rows, the body flexes, gap
//! and inset travel through the composition spec. No second layout system;
//! the runtime keeps focus, pointer and modals.

/// Configure the outer composition adapter while keeping shell geometry
/// inside this block's single chrome resolver.
/// The spec configures the outer one-child composition, not the spacing
/// between header, body and footer. Configure internal spacing with
/// [`Chrome::gap`] before creating the slots.

//! Status line ownership: one message, its tone, its time to live and the
//! job that owns it.
//!
//! Ported from PR #26 `src/shell/status.rs` (commit `f4867e9b5`). The tone is
//! the shared [`Role`] vocabulary instead of the source's `Tone` enum, so the
//! status message renders through the same theme roles as every component:
//! `Tone::Error` lands on [`Role::Danger`], `Tone::Success` on
//! [`Role::Success`], `Tone::Normal` on `Role::Fg(FgStep::Primary)` and
//! `Tone::Secondary` on `Role::Fg(FgStep::Secondary)`.

/// Identity of a long-running job that owns the status message.
///
/// A progress message ends when its job ends, not when another message
/// happens to arrive.

/// The shell-owned status line: at most one message with a tone, a payload,
/// a time to live and an owning job.
///
/// Informational messages expire after [`StatusLine::DEFAULT_TTL_MS`] on the
/// feedback clock; error messages persist until the next acting input.

//! The shell's Esc vocabulary (ported from PR #26 `tests/esc_vocabulary.rs`,
//! commit `f4867e9b5`, scoped to the shell crate): Esc never quits, and the
//! notes the shell writes come from one standard phrase set.
//!
//! The source's test scanned every app's rendered hints; the shell crate owns
//! only its own vocabulary, so the floor here covers the shell's defaults and
//! the quit keys that answer Esc.

//! Write-side kitty keyboard-protocol negotiation for a terminal session.
//!
//! Ported from PR #26 `src/core/keyboard.rs` session plumbing (commit
//! `f4867e9b5`). The sequences come from
//! [`termrock_core::keys::keyboard`]; this module only writes them.
//! Negotiation is opt-in: a session that never calls
//! [`KeyboardNegotiation::enter`] emits no protocol bytes, which keeps the
//! frozen apps byte-identical (the baseline captures contain no
//! negotiation, and the boot mode check pins only 1049/1003/1006/2004).

/// A session's keyboard-protocol negotiation state.
///
/// `enter` pushes once, `leave` pops once (suspend), `resume` pushes again
/// (continue). Every call writes at most one sequence and flushes.

/// Negotiate against `policy` and push if enabled. `detect` is the
/// caller's capability probe (a `CSI ?u` query and reply parse for a
/// real terminal). Returns whether a flag set was pushed.
